  "mechanisms": [
    "Rooms with higher ceilings facilitate greater levels of visuospatial exploration, attention, and navigation, activating dorsal stream regions (precuneus, middle frontal gyrus).",
    "Open spaces, embodying perceived visual and locomotive permeability, activate lateral temporal cortex (middle/superior temporal gyrus) related to processing abstract representations derived from visual motion information.",
    "Enclosed spaces, by reducing perceived visual and locomotive permeability, elicit an emotional reaction (fear) and activate the anterior midcingulate cortex (aMCC), which has direct projections from the amygdala, leading to exit decisions."
  ],
  "moderators": [],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Architectural design elements like ceiling height and perceived enclosure influence aesthetic judgments and approach-avoidance decisions.",
    "Higher ceilings are preferred and judged as more beautiful, potentially due to facilitating visuospatial exploration.",
    "Open spaces are judged as more beautiful, possibly linked to perceived visual motion.",
    "Enclosed spaces are more likely to elicit avoidance decisions, potentially due to fear-related emotional responses."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "A2_Spatial_Scale",
